  "content": {
    "id": "p34-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Consider all functions\n$$f:\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}\\to\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}.$$\nDetermine how many such functions are strictly increasing.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\binom{m}{n}$ if $n\\le m$; $0$ if $n>m$.\n\nChoose the $n$ distinct outputs; their increasing order determines the function uniquely.",
      "worked": "Strict increase requires\n$$f(1)<f(2)<\\cdots<f(n).$$\nChoose $n$ distinct elements of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$. Their smallest element must be $f(1)$, their second smallest must be $f(2)$, and so on. Each choice therefore gives exactly one strictly increasing function, and every strictly increasing function arises this way.\n\nHence there are $\\binom{m}{n}$ such functions when $n\\le m$. When $n>m$, there are insufficient distinct outputs, so there are no such functions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 35
  },
